# WNPM

WNPM is the official package manager and scripting engine of the **WNL (Web Native Layer)** ecosystem.

It was originally developed for **WLite**, where it operates entirely inside a virtual operating environment, but it can also be used as a fully standalone CLI package manager for native Node.js projects.

Unlike traditional package managers, WNPM is designed around a **Virtual File System (VFS)**. Packages can be downloaded, extracted, indexed, and executed without relying on a conventional project directory structure. Depending on the execution mode, WNPM either works entirely inside the isolated WNL storage or behaves like a lightweight bridge to the public npm ecosystem.

Its architecture combines package management, archive extraction, dependency resolution, script execution, and temporary runtime environments into a single unified engine.

---

# ⚠ Integration Notice

## WLite

WNPM is already fully integrated into **WLite**.

There is **nothing to install or configure**. Every WLite instance already includes the complete package manager, WNPX runtime, dependency resolver, and package registry integration.

Do **not** install WNPM inside WLite again.

---

## Webit

Future versions of **Webit** will also include WNPM by default.

Just like WLite, the package manager will already be available as part of the operating environment.

---

## Standalone Mode

Outside of the WNL ecosystem, WNPM can also operate as a completely independent CLI application.

This makes it useful as:

- a lightweight alternative to npm
- a package downloader
- a TGZ archive extractor
- an emergency package installer
- a CLI runner through WNPX
- a scripting engine
- a dependency inspector

When executed natively, WNPM either downloads packages directly from the official npm Registry API or delegates execution to the locally installed Node.js/npm environment whenever native execution is more appropriate.

---

# Features

- Native integration with the WNL Virtual File System (VFS)
- Direct package downloads from the official npm Registry
- Built-in WNPX runtime
- Automatic package.json generation
- Automatic dependency management
- Local and global installations
- Google Drive virtual storage support
- MongoDB virtual storage support
- Binary package execution
- Cross-platform CLI
- Linux, macOS and Windows support
- Automatic project initialization
- Temporary execution environments
- Self-creating directory architecture
- Automatic module extraction
- TAR / TGZ archive processing
- Native Node.js compatibility
- Virtual isolated package execution
- Zero configuration inside WLite

---

# Core Capabilities

## Native VFS Integration

All packages are downloaded, unpacked and stored inside the WNL Virtual File System without directly modifying the host operating system.

Depending on the selected storage backend, modules may exist entirely inside MongoDB, Google Drive or another virtual provider.

---

## Direct npm Registry Access

WNPM communicates directly with the official npm Registry API.

Package metadata is requested in real time before downloading the corresponding compressed archive (.tgz).

No mirrors or third-party registries are required.

---

## Built-in WNPX

The integrated **WNPX** runtime allows packages to be executed without manually installing them into the current project.

If a requested package does not exist locally, WNPX automatically creates a temporary runtime environment, downloads the package, resolves its dependencies and immediately transfers execution to its CLI entry point.

---

## package.json Management

WNPM automatically creates, updates and maintains project manifests.

Dependencies and devDependencies are synchronized during installation and removal operations without requiring manual editing.

---

## Self-Creating Architecture

WNPM automatically creates missing project components whenever required.

This includes:

- package.json
- virtual directories
- dependency trees
- module storage
- temporary execution environments
- internal metadata

No manual initialization beyond the project entry point is required.

---

# Installation

## Using WLite

No installation is required.

WNPM is already bundled with every WLite installation.

Simply open the WLite terminal and start using:

```bash
wnpm
```

or

```bash
wnpx
```

---

## Native CLI Installation

When using WNPM outside WLite, no global npm package is required.

The repository already contains installation scripts inside the **scripts/** directory.

Simply execute the appropriate script for your operating system.

### Linux / macOS

Run:

```bash
scripts/install.sh
```

This script will:

- create `~/.local/bin` if necessary;
- make the CLI executables runnable;
- create symbolic links for `wnpm` and `wnpx`;
- register both commands globally for the current user.

### Linux / macOS

The repository already contains the required installation script.

Run:

```bash
chmod +x scripts/install.sh
./scripts/install.sh
```

The script automatically:

- creates the `~/.local/bin` directory if necessary;
- makes the CLI executables executable;
- creates symbolic links for `wnpm` and `wnpx`;
- registers both commands for the current user.

<details>
<summary>View installation script</summary>

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Create the ~/.local/bin folder if it doesn't exist
mkdir -p ~/.local/bin

# Get the absolute path to the project folder
PROJECT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)"

# Make files executable
chmod +x "$PROJECT_DIR/dist/index.js"
chmod +x "$PROJECT_DIR/dist/wnpx.js"

# Recreate symlinks
ln -sf "$PROJECT_DIR/dist/index.js" ~/.local/bin/wnpm
ln -sf "$PROJECT_DIR/dist/wnpx.js" ~/.local/bin/wnpx

echo "✅ [wnpm] wnpm and wnpx commands are bound to ~/.local/bin!"
```

</details>

---

### Windows

The repository also includes an installation script for Windows.

Run:

```bat
scripts\install.bat
```

The script automatically:

- creates `%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin` if it does not already exist;
- generates Windows command wrappers for `wnpm` and `wnpx`;
- binds both commands for native CLI usage.

> **Note**
>
> Make sure `%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin` is included in your **PATH** environment variable.

<details>
<summary>View installation script</summary>

```bat
@echo off
...
```

</details>

---

## Verify Installation

After running the installation script, restart your terminal and execute:

```bash
wnpm --version
```

or

```bash
wnpx --help
```

If the commands execute successfully, WNPM has been installed correctly and is ready for native CLI usage.

---

# CLI Commands

WNPM provides a familiar command-line interface inspired by the npm ecosystem while operating entirely within the WNL runtime or as a standalone native CLI.

| Command | Alias | Description |
|----------|-------|-------------|
| `wnpm init` | — | Creates a new `package.json` manifest in the current directory. |
| `wnpm install <package>` | `wnpm i` | Downloads and installs a package from the npm Registry. |
| `wnpm install -g <package>` | — | Installs a package globally into the WNL system directory. |
| `wnpm uninstall <package>` | `wnpm rm` | Removes an installed package and updates the project manifest. |
| `wnpm list` | `wnpm ls` | Displays the dependency tree of the current project. |
| `wnpx <package> [args]` | — | Downloads (if necessary) and immediately executes a CLI package. |

---

## Examples

Initialize a new project

```bash
wnpm init
```

Install a package

```bash
wnpm install express
```

Using the short alias

```bash
wnpm i express
```

Install a package globally

```bash
wnpm install -g typescript
```

Remove a dependency

```bash
wnpm rm express
```

List installed dependencies

```bash
wnpm ls
```

Execute a package without installing it permanently

```bash
wnpx create-vite my-app
```

Run a CLI package with custom arguments

```bash
wnpx eslint src --fix
```

---

# Architecture & Operation

Unlike traditional package managers that operate directly on the host file system, WNPM is designed around a modular execution pipeline capable of working both inside the WNL Virtual File System and in a native Node.js environment.

Every package installation passes through a series of well-defined stages that ensure consistent package resolution, extraction, dependency management and runtime execution.

```
User Command
      │
      ▼
 Resolve Package Metadata
      │
      ▼
  npm Registry API
      │
      ▼
 Download .tgz Archive
      │
      ▼
  Stream Extraction
      │
      ▼
 Build Virtual File Tree
      │
      ▼
 Update package.json
      │
      ▼
 Store Package
(MongoDB / Google Drive / Native)
      │
      ▼
 Ready for Execution
```

---

## Package Resolution

When a package is requested, WNPM first communicates with the official npm Registry API.

The latest package metadata is retrieved, including available versions, dependencies, integrity hashes and the direct download URL of the compressed archive.

Only official registry metadata is used.

---

## Archive Download

After resolving package information, WNPM downloads the corresponding `.tgz` archive.

Instead of relying on manual extraction, the archive is processed as a binary stream, allowing packages to be unpacked efficiently while preserving their complete directory structure.

---

## Virtual File System

Inside WLite, extracted files are not written directly to the operating system.

Instead, WNPM reconstructs the package as a hierarchy of Virtual File System nodes.

This allows packages to exist entirely inside the isolated WNL environment while remaining fully accessible to the runtime.

Depending on the configured backend, package data can be stored in:

- MongoDB
- Google Drive
- Native storage

---

## Dependency Management

After extraction, WNPM analyzes the package manifest and synchronizes project dependencies.

If required, the engine automatically creates:

- `package.json`
- dependency records
- virtual module directories
- internal metadata
- temporary runtime structures

No manual configuration is required.

---

## WNPX Runtime

WNPX extends WNPM with temporary package execution.

When a requested executable is not available locally, WNPX automatically:

1. downloads the requested package;
2. resolves its dependencies;
3. creates an isolated execution environment;
4. locates the executable entry point;
5. transfers execution;
6. removes temporary resources when execution finishes.

This enables one-time execution without permanently installing packages.

---

## Native Execution

Outside the WNL ecosystem, WNPM behaves as a standalone CLI package manager.

Native execution allows WNPM to interact directly with the local Node.js runtime while continuing to use the same package resolution pipeline.

This makes WNPM suitable for everyday development workflows, emergency package recovery and standalone archive extraction.

---

## Storage Providers

The storage layer is intentionally abstract.

Current implementations support:

- Google Drive
- MongoDB
- Local filesystem

Additional storage providers can be integrated without changing the package manager itself.

---

# Compatibility

WNPM is designed to operate across multiple environments while maintaining a consistent command-line experience.

| Environment | Status |
|-------------|--------|
| WLite | ✅ Fully Integrated |
| WNL | ✅ Supported |
| Native Node.js | ✅ Supported |
| Linux | ✅ Supported |
| macOS | ✅ Supported |
| Windows | ✅ Supported |
| Google Drive Storage | ✅ Supported |
| MongoDB Storage | ✅ Supported |
| npm Registry | ✅ Supported |
| Webit | 🚧 Planned Integration |

The standalone CLI shares the same package resolution pipeline as the WNL runtime, making it suitable for both native development and virtual operating environments.

---

# Project Structure

```
.
├── dist/               # Compiled CLI binaries
├── node_modules/
├── scripts/            # Native installation scripts
├── src/                # Source code
├── package.json
├── tsconfig.json
├── README.md
└── LICENSE
```

The repository is intentionally organized around a modular architecture, separating the CLI runtime, package resolution logic, installation scripts and platform-specific integrations.

